  "previous_summary": "A phase 2 trial treated 13 people with non-penetrating spinal cord injuries using an infusion of their own bone-marrow stem cells. By six months, all but one had sensory or motor improvements, and no adverse effects were reported. The researchers called for larger follow-up studies while noting that some recovery could have occurred spontaneously.",
  "previous_claim_decider": "rule",
  "corrected_summary": "A phase 2 case series gave 13 people with spinal cord injuries a single intravenous infusion of their own bone-marrow mesenchymal cells expanded in autologous serum. Twelve improved on the ASIA scale by six months. The uncontrolled study could not exclude spontaneous recovery, placebo effects or observer bias."
